  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec\u2061x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph about the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $sec\u2061x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061(-x)=sec\u2061x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec\u2061(-x) & =sec\u2061x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
